<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wandsbek</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wandsbek is a borough in the north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. A Holstein estate and town under Danish rule and later Prussian, it was known in the 18th century as the home of the poet Matthias <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, who published the newspaper Der Wandsbecker Bote there. It was incorporated into Hamburg by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-hamburg-act">Greater Hamburg Act</a> of 1937.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wandsbek.jpg" alt="Wandsbek" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Staro1 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hh-Stormarnhaus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
